{"name":"First Waltz","hex":"#d5bcb2","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"first","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Preceding all others of a series or kind; the ordinal of one; earliest.","Most eminent or exalted; most excellent; chief; highest."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Before anything else; firstly.","For the first time."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/first"},{"term":"waltz","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A ballroom dance in 3/4 time.","A piece of music for this dance (or in triple time)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To dance the waltz (with).","To move briskly and unhesitatingly, especially in an inappropriately casual manner, or when unannounced or uninvited."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/waltz"}],"entries":[{"title":"Waltz","blocks":[{"text":"The waltz is a ballroom and folk dance, in triple time, performed primarily in closed position, originating from German-speaking regions of Europe in the 18th century. Along with the ländler and allemande, the waltz was sometimes referred to by the generic term German Dance in publications during the late 18th and early 19th centuries."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Phenakistoscope_3g07690d.gif/330px-Phenakistoscope_3g07690d.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waltz"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Waltz1816_72.jpg/500px-Waltz1816_72.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Waltz1816_72.jpg/500px-Waltz1816_72.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Detail from frontispiece to Thomas Wilson Correct Method of German and French Waltzing (1816), showing nine positions of the waltz, clockwise from the left (the musicians are at far left). At that time, the waltz was a relatively new dance in England, and the fact that it was a couples dance (as opposed to the traditional group dances), and that the gentleman clasped his arm around the lady's waist, gave it a dubious moral status.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWaltz1816_72.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/La_Valse.jpg/500px-La_Valse.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/La_Valse.jpg/500px-La_Valse.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Waltz, by Camille Claudel (cast in 1905)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALa_Valse.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Man_and_woman_dancing_a_waltz_%281887%29.gif/500px-Man_and_woman_dancing_a_waltz_%281887%29.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Man_and_woman_dancing_a_waltz_%281887%29.gif/500px-Man_and_woman_dancing_a_waltz_%281887%29.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Man and woman dancing a waltz by Eadweard Muybridge, 1887","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMan_and_woman_dancing_a_waltz_(1887).gif"}],"sounds":{"first":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9e/En-us-first.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/9/9e/En-us-first.ogg/En-us-first.ogg.mp3"}],"waltz":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/ba/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-waltz.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia